data.content:
Here's what breaks in EF Core 10 and how to fix each piece:

---

## 1. `ExecuteUpdateAsync` expression tree — won't compile

**What changed:** `ExecuteUpdateAsync` now takes a plain `Func<>` delegate instead of `Expression<Func<>>`. Your manual expression-tree construction no longer compiles.

**Fix — use the new imperative lambda:**

```csharp
await context.Blogs.ExecuteUpdateAsync(s =>
{
    s.SetProperty(b => b.Views, 8);
    if (nameChanged)
    {
        s.SetProperty(b => b.Name, "foo");
    }
});
```

No more `Expression.Call` / `Expression.Lambda` gymnastics needed.

---

## 2. Complex type columns — silent column sharing → uniquified names

**What changed:** Two complex properties with the same member name (e.g. `ShippingAddress.Street` and `BillingAddress.Street`) now get auto-suffixed (`Street`, `Street1`). Nested complex types now include the full path (`Complex_NestedComplex_Property` instead of just `NestedComplex_Property`). This generates a new migration that renames columns.

**Fix — pin column names explicitly to preserve the existing schema:**

```csharp
modelBuilder.Entity<Customer>(b =>
{
    b.ComplexProperty(c => c.ShippingAddress, p =>
        p.Property(a => a.Street).HasColumnName("ShippingAddress_Street"));
    b.ComplexProperty(c => c.BillingAddress, p =>
        p.Property(a => a.Street).HasColumnName("BillingAddress_Street"));
});

// Nested: pin full-path columns to old names
modelBuilder.Entity<Order>()
    .ComplexProperty(o => o.Payment)
    .ComplexProperty(p => p.BillingAddress)
    .Property(a => a.City)
    .HasColumnName("BillingAddress_City"); // old: NestedComplex_Property
```

---

## 3. `IRelationalCommandDiagnosticsLogger` — new `logCommandText` parameter

**What changed:** All `CommandReaderExecuting` / `CommandReaderExecuted` / `CommandScalarExecuting` etc. methods now require an additional `string logCommandText` parameter (redacted SQL for logging).

**Fix:**

```csharp
public class MyLogger : IRelationalCommandDiagnosticsLogger
{
    public InterceptionResult<DbDataReader> CommandReaderExecuting(
        IRelationalConnection connection,
        DbCommand command,
        DbContext context,
        Guid commandId,
        Guid connectionId,
        DateTimeOffset startTime,
        string logCommandText)          // ← new parameter
    {
        // Use logCommandText (sensitive data redacted) for logging
        // Use command.CommandText for the real SQL
    }
}
```

---

**TL;DR:** The biggest win is #1 — delete the expression-tree code entirely and use the new imperative `ExecuteUpdateAsync` lambda. For #2 and #3, add explicit column names and the new `logCommandText` parameter respectively.
